reviews.explanation:
The cyclic derivative identity gives (∂P/∂V)ₜ(∂V/∂T)ₚ(∂T/∂P)ᵥ = −1 wherever the derivatives exist. The source illustrates it with PV = RT on a unit amount basis:
P = RT/V ⇒ (∂P/∂V)ₜ = −RT/V²;
V = RT/P ⇒ (∂V/∂T)ₚ = R/P;
T = PV/R ⇒ (∂T/∂P)ᵥ = V/R.
Their product is (−RT/V²)(R/P)(V/R) = −RT/(PV) = −1. Therefore (c). The identity itself is more general than an ideal gas.
